Target Layer: Core Engine Infrastructure

1. Executive Summary
Long-running AI agents executing multi-hour, multi-step code generation tasks inevitably suffer from compounding multi-level drift. This manifests across seven distinct planes: Business, System, Architecture, Design, Implementation, Deployment, and Maintenance.
Current agent frameworks rely heavily on localized, mono-loop prompt engineering, which fails to isolate or remediate structural drift. This RFC proposes an open-source, deterministic, Kubernetes-style Control Loop framework based on an Observer-Corrector paradigm. It isolates the execution engine from multi-layered guardrails via a uniform interface contract, allowing for decoupled, incremental system expansion.

2. Architectural Design Patterns
The Control Loop Topology
Instead of treating an LLM agent as a singular monolithic brain, this framework treats it as an un-trusted actor operating within a closed loop. The loop relies on three decoupled primitives:
Observe: Non-destructively inspects the workspace state (Codebase, ASTs, PRDs, Staging Env Logs).
Evaluate: Measures the delta between the observed state and the original baseline specifications.
Correct: Generates remediation plans and instructs execution agents or tools to execute changes when drift crosses defined thresholds.
