3. Technical Specification & Interfaces
To maintain strict modularity, all drift guardrails across the seven systemic layers must implement a uniform lifecycle interface contract.
The Unified DriftGuard Contract
export type DriftLayer = 
  | 'business' 
  | 'system' 
  | 'architecture' 
  | 'design' 
  | 'implementation' 
  | 'deployment' 
  | 'maintenance';

export interface StateSnapshot {
  timestamp: number;
  layer: DriftLayer;
  payload: Record<string, any>; // Extensible metadata specific to the layer
}

export interface DriftReport {
  hasDrifted: boolean;
  severity: 'low' | 'medium' | 'high' | 'critical';
  varianceDescription: string;
  telemetryDelta: any;
}

export interface CorrectionPlan {
  strategy: 'rollback' | 'refactor' | 'halt' | 'override';
  instructionSet: string; // Structured payload fed back to the LLM/Tool context
}

export interface DriftGuard {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly targetLayer: DriftLayer;

  /** Inspects the environment to harvest the current reality */
  observe(workspacePath: string, contextState: any): Promise<StateSnapshot>;

  /** Compares reality against historical boundaries/specifications */
  evaluate(current: StateSnapshot, baseline: any): Promise<DriftReport>;

  /** Emits instructions or hooks to correct the system vector */
  correct(report: DriftReport): Promise<CorrectionPlan>;
}



4. The 7-Layer Drift Taxonomy (Phased Implementation Roadmap)
We propose an inside-out evolutionary rollout. We start at the code layer (Phase 1) and expand outward to systemic boundaries.
Phase 1: Core Engine & Keyboard Operations
Layer 5: Implementation Drift: Catches syntactic bugs, logic compilation errors, and local infinite code-generation loop regressions.
Layer 4: Design Drift: Utilizes Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) evaluation hooks to prevent code smells (e.g., breaking SOLID principles, bloated cyclomatic complexity).
Phase 2: System Boundaries & Integration
Layer 3: Architectural Drift: Enforces system boundaries (e.g., stopping an agent from introducing unauthorized third-party paradigms or crossing strict microservice boundaries).
Layer 6: Deployment Drift: Observes ephemeral staging environments to catch configurations, schema migrations, and Dockerfile drifts during autonomous code deployments.
Phase 3: Alignment & Lifecycle Management
Layer 1: Business Drift: Continuously evaluates functional feature code drift against natural language Product Requirement Documents (PRDs).
Layer 2: System Drift: Tracks non-functional requirements (such as token budget creep, performance latency limits, and security vulnerabilities).
Layer 7: Maintenance Drift: Blocks structural decay like unpinned dependencies, outdated lockfiles, and code deprecation.

5. Architectural Trade-offs & Challenges (Open Questions)
We invite the engineering community to contribute immediately to solving these fundamental design tensions:
State Minimization vs. Context Bloat: How do we record state snapshots spanning hundreds of steps without overflowing LLM context limits or spiking cost vectors?
Observer Determinism: Should the Observe and Evaluate steps be purely deterministic (e.g., running AST parsers, linters, testing frameworks) or semi-probabilistic (using cheaper, highly focused LLMs)?
Halt Strategies: At what severity threshold of drift does an agent loop automatically freeze execution to raise human-in-the-loop paging events?

6. How to Contribute
We are designing this system entirely in the open prior to finalizing code frameworks. To contribute:
Review: Open an issue or submit a Pull Request to refine this core RFC contract.
Pick a Layer: Propose individual specifications under rfcs/layers/ for specific drift guards (e.g., how an AST design guard should operate).
